{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon's experiment with decriminalizing drugs is coming to an end as a new law takes effect, making it a crime once again to possess small amounts of hard drugs. This new law gives individuals caught with illicit drugs the option to either face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option, known as a \"deflection program,\" aims to divert people from the criminal justice system and towards addiction and mental health services. However, this program is not mandatory for counties, which may lead to inconsistent policies across different regions. Supporters of the law argue that this allows counties to develop programs tailored to their resources.\n\n*** \nThe criteria for eligibility in the deflection program are strict, requiring individuals to have no other charges, warrants, or history of violent behavior, and to be medically stable. If an individual meets these requirements, officers will contact a mobile behavioral health outreach team to connect them with services. However, if the team cannot respond within 30 minutes, the individual will be taken to jail.\n\n*** \nThe new law introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public areas and makes personal-use possession a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail. P.P.S As people of all age groups are affected by this law, it is essential to consider the impact on the entire country and how it may influence daily activities, such as shopping or eating out, and even simple actions like trying to eat or speak with a dry tongue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.004175516311079264}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.6931508779525757}, {"token": " experiment", "logprob": -0.3204382658004761}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -2.